WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:02.133
- [Sara] So, the way
that this is gonna work is

00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:05.000
I'll open it up with a few
directed questions for you,

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.033
Gary, and then
after about, you know,

00:00:07.033 --> 00:00:08.934
15 minutes, or
whenever we get really bored,

00:00:08.934 --> 00:00:11.133
we're gonna move
into the audience

00:00:11.133 --> 00:00:12.834
using the crowd mic app--

00:00:12.834 --> 00:00:15.233
- Did you just say
that I was gonna be boring

00:00:15.233 --> 00:00:16.433
in the first 15 minutes?

00:00:16.433 --> 00:00:17.066
- Yes.

00:00:17.066 --> 00:00:19.300
- No, I said that after we
get bored with my questions,

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.400
we're gonna move on
to audience questions.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:23.934
But, I'm glad that
you brought that up

00:00:23.934 --> 00:00:27.433
because I think that
by inviting you here,

00:00:27.433 --> 00:00:30.333
especially to kickoff,
this is kind of where

00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:34.834
we stop being polite and we
start getting real, right?

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:39.967
My first question for you is
kind of a generic question,

00:00:39.967 --> 00:00:42.533
but, the question is "how
powerful is the combination

00:00:42.533 --> 00:00:44.633
"of purpose and innovation?"

00:00:44.633 --> 00:00:46.200
I'm asking this
because I think that

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.033
we need to hold
ourselves accountable.

00:00:48.033 --> 00:00:50.867
Like, is this just another
cheesy conference theme

00:00:50.867 --> 00:00:53.667
that doesn't mean anything,
or does it actually mean,

00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:57.834
like, from your perspective,
what's the power in that combo?

00:00:59.967 --> 00:01:02.100
- Yeah, it's both.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.433
It's a cheesy conference
theme, and it's real, right?

00:01:05.433 --> 00:01:08.533
I mean, I think,
at the end of the day,

00:01:08.533 --> 00:01:12.033
innovation, purpose,
these are very clear.

00:01:12.033 --> 00:01:15.667
I mean, it's kind of
like the value of oxygen

00:01:15.667 --> 00:01:19.233
or, like, of course,

00:01:19.233 --> 00:01:21.066
anybody who's sitting here

00:01:21.066 --> 00:01:24.433
that doesn't think that
innovation matters or disrupts

00:01:24.433 --> 00:01:28.033
or is a key of how
we evolve as people,

00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:30.533
or that it's a
hell of a lot better

00:01:30.533 --> 00:01:33.633
to have a purpose behind
what you're doing than not,

00:01:33.633 --> 00:01:38.467
is tone-deaf to the
reality of what creates upside

00:01:38.467 --> 00:01:41.633
both as a human
and as a business.

00:01:41.633 --> 00:01:44.300
- [Sara] Yeah, so then it's
just about implementation.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:45.767
How do we do this, and so--

00:01:45.767 --> 00:01:47.200
- Meaning execution?

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:48.767
- [Sara] Yeah, execution,
implementation--

00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:50.834
- So, I know a lot of
you don't know who I am,

00:01:50.834 --> 00:01:53.767
unless you saw me two years ago.

00:01:53.767 --> 00:01:56.367
I get a lot of flak
in the technology space.

00:01:56.367 --> 00:01:58.900
I was an early investor in
Facebook, and Twitter, and Uber,

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:00.967
and did really well, and
have a lot of street-cred

00:02:00.967 --> 00:02:03.533
in that world, and in
that world, all of them

00:02:03.533 --> 00:02:04.934
are super upset with me because

00:02:04.934 --> 00:02:07.800
I always say
that ideas are crap.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:12.667
Ideas are crap because
I get 10,000 emails a week

00:02:12.667 --> 00:02:14.767
from people that want me
to invest in their companies

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:15.834
and they tell me that they have

00:02:15.834 --> 00:02:17.800
the greatest idea of all time.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.133
Everybody in here has an idea.

00:02:20.133 --> 00:02:22.567
The amount of times somebody
comes up to me and goes,

00:02:22.567 --> 00:02:25.900
"Oh, I had the Uber
idea before it happened!"

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:29.233
Great, what do
you want, a cookie?

00:02:29.233 --> 00:02:33.633
Like, ideas are
completely worthless

00:02:33.633 --> 00:02:36.066
unless you execute
against those ideas

00:02:36.066 --> 00:02:39.767
and you can sit here and
tell me what your purpose is,

00:02:39.767 --> 00:02:41.934
but unless you go
and execute against it,

00:02:41.934 --> 00:02:44.066
it's no different than
something like an idea,

00:02:44.066 --> 00:02:45.633
which is completely meaningless

00:02:45.633 --> 00:02:47.967
without fundamental execution.

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:50.867
- [Sara] Okay, alright, so a lot
of here do have ideas--

00:02:50.867 --> 00:02:51.700
- Great!

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:55.467
- But, one of the
biggest roadblocks,

00:02:55.467 --> 00:02:57.333
and we do want cookies, too!

00:02:57.333 --> 00:02:58.467
But, one of the biggest
roadblocks is--

00:02:58.467 --> 00:03:00.500
- [Gary] By the way, on that
note, I do this a lot.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:02.767
That setup in the back with
the candies and the stuff,

00:03:02.767 --> 00:03:04.133
that's pretty legit,

00:03:04.133 --> 00:03:07.500
so we should clap
up the candy selection.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:08.667
I'm into that.

00:03:09.734 --> 00:03:10.934
- I didn't even see that setup.

00:03:10.934 --> 00:03:13.266
- It's yummy back
there, I'm telling you.

00:03:13.266 --> 00:03:14.300
- Alright, so if you
want a piece of candy

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:15.934
because you have
ideas, that's your spot,

00:03:15.934 --> 00:03:18.900
but, what if
that's a roadblock for us.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:21.266
Like, how do we get other
people onboard with our ideas?

00:03:21.266 --> 00:03:22.467
- You mean
within the organization?

00:03:22.467 --> 00:03:23.633
- [Sara] Right, because
a lot of these folks

00:03:23.633 --> 00:03:24.834
are gonna go home in three days

00:03:24.834 --> 00:03:25.734
they're gonna be pumped up

00:03:25.734 --> 00:03:27.000
about the things
they've heard here

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.100
and, like Sam mentioned,
everyone's gonna say,

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:31.700
"Oh, you went to
that conference."

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:34.934
- Yeah, so, over
the last seven years

00:03:34.934 --> 00:03:37.500
I built my family's liquor
store into one of the largest

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:39.300
ecommerce wine
businesses in America,

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.100
I went out and
started investing in companies

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:42.400
that did well, and
then seven years ago,

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:45.367
I decided that I was gonna
build a client service agency

00:03:45.367 --> 00:03:46.900
to help the biggest
brands in the world

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:48.867
understand where
the world was going,

00:03:48.867 --> 00:03:50.400
how to market, you know,

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:52.333
the days of 
making TV commercials

00:03:52.333 --> 00:03:54.300
and print advertising
are not dead,

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.700
but they're waning,
they're very overpriced,

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.600
by comparison to some of the
opportunities that we see

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:00.934
in Facebook environments
and things of that nature,

00:04:00.934 --> 00:04:02.734
and the thing
that I didn't know,

00:04:02.734 --> 00:04:04.400
because I'm an entrepreneur,

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.033
when I make all my decisions,
I just have to go home,

00:04:07.033 --> 00:04:08.934
look at myself in
the mirror, make a call,

00:04:08.934 --> 00:04:10.433
and then go and move.

00:04:10.433 --> 00:04:13.266
I've had to deploy an
enormous amount of empathy

00:04:13.266 --> 00:04:16.033
to understand how many of
you don't have that ability,

00:04:16.033 --> 00:04:18.266
and, so, there's a
lot of different ways

00:04:18.266 --> 00:04:20.899
to answer your question and
I used to be really frustrated

00:04:20.899 --> 00:04:22.367
with everybody here
because I didn't think

00:04:22.367 --> 00:04:24.700
they had enough
gusto to go home and do it.

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:26.934
Now, I'm at a place
where I mainly blame the CEOs

00:04:26.934 --> 00:04:28.800
of all the companies
that they work for,

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:33.266
or the board, or Wall Street
for creating 90 day behavior

00:04:33.266 --> 00:04:35.600
because everybody maps
toward their stock price.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:36.967
There's a million
different ways to go about it.

00:04:36.967 --> 00:04:40.633
Look, I think the
number one thing you can do

00:04:40.633 --> 00:04:43.767
is actually the best
advice I have for human beings,

00:04:43.767 --> 00:04:46.333
which is communicate,
whether it's your spouse,

00:04:46.333 --> 00:04:47.834
or your children,
or your parents,

00:04:47.834 --> 00:04:50.767
or something you care about,
communication is the game.

00:04:50.767 --> 00:04:54.700
Figuring out how to
be politically correct

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:57.934
is not the answer,
figuring out how to tell people

00:04:57.934 --> 00:05:00.133
what you believe the
company should be doing

00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:03.100
in a respectful manner,
that's the game.

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:06.533
So, look, my belief is that
98% of the people in this room

00:05:06.533 --> 00:05:09.000
can't do anything with
what they learn this weekend.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:09.834
I mean it.

00:05:09.834 --> 00:05:12.000
It's not fun to
say, but it's true.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:13.266
I now work with
enough big companies

00:05:13.266 --> 00:05:14.900
and I understand,
and more importantly,

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:17.033
the stuff that
I most believe in,

00:05:17.033 --> 00:05:19.500
look, I think the one
thing we're all grounded in

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:22.767
is the end consumer's attention.

00:05:22.767 --> 00:05:24.400
You have a branding issue.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:26.000
We talked about
this two years ago.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:28.333
I'm here two years later,
I looked at the Google searches,

00:05:28.333 --> 00:05:30.000
I looked at the
social media mentions,

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.400
people still don't
know what credit unions are.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:34.200
Like, let's cut to the chase.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.767
You have a comms
issue as an industry,

00:05:36.767 --> 00:05:38.767
let alone as your
individual companies,

00:05:38.767 --> 00:05:41.400
and, so, the really sad part,

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:42.867
and we have
financial service clients

00:05:42.867 --> 00:05:45.000
and alcohol brands,
and government stuff,

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.033
like, I work with this.

00:05:47.033 --> 00:05:48.600
There's way too
many people in this room,

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:51.266
and definitely the
logos that they work for,

00:05:51.266 --> 00:05:53.934
that use compliance as an excuse

00:05:53.934 --> 00:05:57.600
for not doing what's
right in a 2016 environment,

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.400
and, so, what should they do?

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:02.333
They should tell
people what they believe

00:06:02.333 --> 00:06:05.467
within their organization,
top, down, left, right.

00:06:05.467 --> 00:06:08.166
The truth is, most
people actually don't know

00:06:08.166 --> 00:06:09.967
what they actually wanna do.

00:06:09.967 --> 00:06:12.767
We're living in a world, right
now, of headline readers.

00:06:12.767 --> 00:06:13.967
The majority of people in here

00:06:13.967 --> 00:06:16.767
that really understand
the tactical executions

00:06:16.767 --> 00:06:19.166
within these new
communication platforms

00:06:19.166 --> 00:06:20.533
are almost non-existent.

00:06:20.533 --> 00:06:22.200
You might have
read an AdAge article,

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:23.166
or the Wall Street Journal,

00:06:23.166 --> 00:06:26.000
about what's happening in
Facebook, SnapChat, Instagram,

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:27.567
but do you really
know how it works?

00:06:27.567 --> 00:06:28.400
- [Sara] Or it's like,

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:29.700
"Oh, we'll just
hire a young person."

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:33.700
- Right, we're gonna hire
up a 24 year old, she knows!

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:35.000
- The problem with
that is that young people

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:37.600
are often inexperienced
with strategy.

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:38.967
- Quick question,
who's got an idea?

00:06:38.967 --> 00:06:41.266
Raise your hand
if you have an idea.

00:06:41.266 --> 00:06:42.967
Come on, I don't believe this.

00:06:42.967 --> 00:06:45.266
No one has an idea?

00:06:45.266 --> 00:06:48.333
Okay, I'm at the
wrong conference.

00:06:48.333 --> 00:06:49.600
- [Sara] That
could be a problem.

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:50.400
- That is a big problem.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.333
- [Sara] Gary,
we're gonna have to rewind.

00:06:52.333 --> 00:06:53.767
How do you get an idea?

00:06:53.767 --> 00:06:55.166
- [Gary] Alright, now look,
I think everybody has an idea.

00:06:55.166 --> 00:06:56.467
Nobody wants to raise their hand

00:06:56.467 --> 00:06:58.734
because they know that I'm
crazy enough to jump out here

00:06:58.734 --> 00:07:01.066
and maybe punch
them in the face.

00:07:01.066 --> 00:07:02.767
So, I have empathy to why they

00:07:02.767 --> 00:07:03.834
didn't wanna raise their hands.

00:07:03.834 --> 00:07:05.300
Go ahead, where were
you gonna go with that?

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:06.567
So, everybody has an idea.

00:07:06.567 --> 00:07:08.567
Let me save you time,
they all have ideas.

00:07:08.567 --> 00:07:09.166
Next.

00:07:09.166 --> 00:07:10.300
- I wanted to let
them leave their hand

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:12.767
if they've actually
told somebody about it.

00:07:12.767 --> 00:07:14.633
- Look, the problem is
it's not their fault.

00:07:14.633 --> 00:07:15.800
I used to think
it was their fault.

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:16.633
It's not their fault.

00:07:16.633 --> 00:07:17.967
They don't work in organizations

00:07:17.967 --> 00:07:20.066
that fester an
environment or a culture

00:07:20.066 --> 00:07:23.000
that actually rewards
that kind of behavior.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:25.734
(audience applause)

00:07:25.734 --> 00:07:28.900
Now, don't use that
as an excuse, though.

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:30.433
You know, because that's
the fun thing to clap on.

00:07:30.433 --> 00:07:33.467
Like, yeah, it's the
man, it's the machine.

00:07:33.467 --> 00:07:35.500
All of you should quit
and start your own companies

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:36.834
if you have such a good idea.

00:07:36.834 --> 00:07:37.834
- Hmm, okay.

00:07:40.533 --> 00:07:43.166
- Don't laugh,
I mean, that's what I did.

00:07:43.166 --> 00:07:44.767
- I believe you.

00:07:44.767 --> 00:07:47.600
- Actually, funny enough,
it was actually your Web 2.0

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:49.266
do what you love talk was

00:07:49.266 --> 00:07:52.567
one of those big triggers.

00:07:52.567 --> 00:07:53.266
One of the things
that's revealing,

00:07:53.266 --> 00:07:54.233
if you think about it,

00:07:54.233 --> 00:07:56.300
is we talk about purpose, and
I open this with this as well,

00:07:56.300 --> 00:07:57.200
it's find your purpose.

00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:59.400
What's the problem
that you find a deep,

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:02.066
intrinsic purpose
and wanna solve it?

00:08:02.066 --> 00:08:03.900
If that means
leaving your credit union,

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:06.967
sorry CEOs out there, but that
might be one of the options.

00:08:06.967 --> 00:08:07.967
- [Gary] You know it's
funny, it's the way,

00:08:07.967 --> 00:08:11.333
it's funny how you
positioned my talk, right?

00:08:11.333 --> 00:08:14.166
We're gonna get real,
it's gonna get uncomfortable.

00:08:14.166 --> 00:08:17.233
My purpose, when
I publicly speak is,

00:08:17.233 --> 00:08:19.200
to be very frank,
I'm just hopeful

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:21.734
that I can break through
to one person in this room.

00:08:21.734 --> 00:08:25.066
I'm very practical
about how this goes.

00:08:25.066 --> 00:08:27.066
I do think people get inspired

00:08:27.066 --> 00:08:28.633
and think
differently in an offsite

00:08:28.633 --> 00:08:29.633
and things of that nature,

00:08:29.633 --> 00:08:31.333
but I have empathy
and understanding,

00:08:31.333 --> 00:08:32.900
and understand
how the world works.

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:34.133
In an entrepreneurial event,

00:08:34.133 --> 00:08:36.332
I actually think a lot
of things can go and happen,

00:08:36.332 --> 00:08:39.033
they just don't have the
friction or the red tape,

00:08:39.033 --> 00:08:40.900
but when you work
within an organization,

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:44.500
it's harder to make change,
especially if you don't run it.

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:48.100
So, I would tell you that
sharing content has worked.

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:50.200
One of the things
that I've seen really work,

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:53.133
like, now, instead of
pontificating and peacocking,

00:08:53.133 --> 00:08:54.934
let me answer this specifically.

00:08:54.934 --> 00:08:56.233
If you really care about this,

00:08:56.233 --> 00:08:58.033
and you believe
in certain theories,

00:08:58.033 --> 00:09:00.900
I would find these
videos and articles online

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:03.800
and I would share them via
email within your organization.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:06.266
Chipping away, like a
woodpecker, over and over

00:09:06.266 --> 00:09:09.033
with that content, has actually
brought a lot of change

00:09:09.033 --> 00:09:10.633
with a lot of big organizations.

00:09:10.633 --> 00:09:13.734
I also think things like this,
but on a company-wide level,

00:09:13.734 --> 00:09:16.100
where you bring in people that
are thinking about the world

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:20.767
differently than you, with
senior leadership at the event,

00:09:20.767 --> 00:09:23.233
has been a very
remarkable way to do change.

00:09:23.233 --> 00:09:25.467
It makes me sad
that my company has done

00:09:25.467 --> 00:09:27.433
two or three or
four years of good work,

00:09:27.433 --> 00:09:29.533
sold a lot of
products for clients,

00:09:29.533 --> 00:09:32.033
but we may move the
needle just a little bit,

00:09:32.033 --> 00:09:34.867
and then maybe one
keynote that I do with leaders

00:09:34.867 --> 00:09:38.800
moves the organization
10X, and so, I don't like that

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:42.333
because I feel like that's
overvaluing sizzle over steak,

00:09:42.333 --> 00:09:44.667
but it's the reality of
how human behavior works,

00:09:44.667 --> 00:09:46.633
so I do think,
if you have the power

00:09:46.633 --> 00:09:49.633
to create offsite events,
or program information

00:09:49.633 --> 00:09:53.400
to senior leadership
in whatever environment,

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:54.433
one thing that somebody took

00:09:54.433 --> 00:09:56.000
from advice from
a talk like this,

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:59.133
she joined the
committee that puts together

00:09:59.133 --> 00:10:00.633
the end of the year offsite,

00:10:00.633 --> 00:10:03.367
and then programmed a bunch of
characters that look like me

00:10:03.367 --> 00:10:05.233
and it was the most
meaningful thing that happened

00:10:05.233 --> 00:10:07.266
to that organization,
they changed their behavior

00:10:07.266 --> 00:10:08.300
and they're having
one of their best years

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:10.834
in 17 years right now as
a publicly traded company.

00:10:10.834 --> 00:10:12.900
So, there's things to do.

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:15.200
It's fun to clap that
the man doesn't let you

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:17.500
and your organization
doesn't support it,

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:19.800
but at the end of the day,

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:22.433
we should really put our
big girl, big boy pants on

00:10:22.433 --> 00:10:24.166
and try to figure out
how to make it happen.

00:10:24.166 --> 00:10:25.900
- That's obviously
to the individuals,

00:10:25.900 --> 00:10:27.300
but a question here
that's come through,

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:28.767
and I want you to
answer this to the CEOs

00:10:28.767 --> 00:10:30.734
of these companies.

00:10:30.734 --> 00:10:32.700
The question is,
how can they foster

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:35.133
more entrepreneurial
culture internally?

00:10:35.133 --> 00:10:36.367
- [Gary] Force it.

00:10:36.367 --> 00:10:38.233
Being a CEO is being a dictator.

00:10:38.233 --> 00:10:39.667
Create the rules.

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:43.734
- [Scott] Simple.

00:10:43.734 --> 00:10:45.000
- [Sara] More questions.

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:47.133
Bring it on.
- Sally, you're the CEO.

00:10:47.133 --> 00:10:48.533
Make the rules.

00:10:48.533 --> 00:10:50.800
Force everybody to
spend 20% of their money

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:52.667
on things that
don't have an ROI,

00:10:52.667 --> 00:10:54.533
that is scored with internal,

00:10:54.533 --> 00:10:56.166
are things that
you've never done before,

00:10:56.166 --> 00:10:58.133
and reward people for
the behavior of spending

00:10:58.133 --> 00:10:59.867
20% of their time
and money on something

00:10:59.867 --> 00:11:03.000
that you can't measure or
that you've done historically.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:03.934
You just solved it.

00:11:03.934 --> 00:11:05.433
There you go.Go do that.

00:11:05.433 --> 00:11:07.233
- [Scott] Simple. So just a
quick reminder, everyone.

00:11:07.233 --> 00:11:08.266
You can ask Gary a question.

00:11:08.266 --> 00:11:09.000
- And by the way, by the way,

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:10.467
I apologize, this
is important to me.

00:11:10.467 --> 00:11:11.533
I'm the CEO of my company.

00:11:11.533 --> 00:11:13.066
I have 650 employees.

00:11:13.066 --> 00:11:16.867
I tell them that
they get scored on culture

00:11:16.867 --> 00:11:19.834
and emotional intelligence
over bringing me money,

00:11:19.834 --> 00:11:22.567
over doing the best work,
duh duh duh duh duh,

00:11:22.567 --> 00:11:23.734
It is the North Star.

00:11:23.734 --> 00:11:26.066
The North Star that
people get rewarded for

00:11:26.066 --> 00:11:28.333
is how they play with
the other girls and boys

00:11:28.333 --> 00:11:30.700
within the organization
because I like continuity,

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:32.300
because continuity
leads to speed.

00:11:32.300 --> 00:11:34.533
I think speed is the most
important thing in business.

00:11:34.533 --> 00:11:37.266
And then when we reward it,
where the nicest people

00:11:37.266 --> 00:11:39.467
get raises and promotions,
even though they may be

00:11:39.467 --> 00:11:42.834
a B talent, and the
guy who's an A talent

00:11:42.834 --> 00:11:46.467
but is a little bit of a douche
bag doesn't get promoted,

00:11:46.467 --> 00:11:47.934
that everybody starts believing

00:11:47.934 --> 00:11:49.300
what I actually care about.

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:51.200
Because being a
CEO is a dictatorship.

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:53.600
So I make the rules,
and then I act on them.

00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:55.300
So go do that.

00:11:55.300 --> 00:11:56.734
- So just a quick
reminder, everyone,

00:11:56.734 --> 00:11:59.900
you can ask Gary questions
through the THINK 16 app,

00:11:59.900 --> 00:12:02.066
and they will come up
here on my little tablet here,

00:12:02.066 --> 00:12:03.100
so if you have a question,

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:05.433
do punch it
through the application.

00:12:05.433 --> 00:12:06.567
Do you have another question?

00:12:06.567 --> 00:12:07.433
- There's no more?

00:12:07.433 --> 00:12:08.834
I feel like--
- No, we're actually

00:12:08.834 --> 00:12:09.867
running out quickly.

00:12:09.867 --> 00:12:11.567
- Oh, I would love for
more crowd questions for this,

00:12:11.567 --> 00:12:12.633
but I--
- One of the traps--

00:12:12.633 --> 00:12:14.133
- Would you jump in the
audience and ask questions?

00:12:14.133 --> 00:12:15.633
Because I think that,
you know, it might be.

00:12:15.633 --> 00:12:17.533
- I'll crowd surf. (laughs)

00:12:17.533 --> 00:12:19.633
- So let me just move it back.

00:12:19.633 --> 00:12:21.367
Because I think a lot of people,

00:12:21.367 --> 00:12:22.667
I see people
nodding their heads, like,

00:12:22.667 --> 00:12:25.166
"Yeah, yeah, we do
have a comms issue."

00:12:25.166 --> 00:12:27.000
What about the
folks who are like,

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:29.333
"The number one
problem in my organization

00:12:29.333 --> 00:12:30.767
"is communications,
but I don't know anything

00:12:30.767 --> 00:12:32.467
"about communications,
it's not my specialty.

00:12:32.467 --> 00:12:34.367
"Or technology, I don't know
anything about technology.

00:12:34.367 --> 00:12:36.600
"I'm an accountant,
I'm an engineer."

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:39.433
You know? I mean,
what about those folks?

00:12:39.433 --> 00:12:41.734
- Well, first,
they may not be right

00:12:41.734 --> 00:12:44.433
about comms being a
problem, because if they're not

00:12:44.433 --> 00:12:45.967
experts in it or understand it,

00:12:45.967 --> 00:12:48.200
they may, as a
focus group of one,

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:50.700
think that that organization
is not doing the best job,

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:52.533
but first they want to
be grounded in actually

00:12:52.533 --> 00:12:54.667
knowing the craft
or what's happening.

00:12:54.667 --> 00:12:56.767
I mean, look, there's
a lot of organizations

00:12:56.767 --> 00:12:59.100
that are getting a lot of
ROI from their traditional

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:01.100
media spends and
things of that nature.

00:13:01.100 --> 00:13:03.233
It's just that they haven't
put themselves in a position

00:13:03.233 --> 00:13:06.934
to test direct mail, or
sales-driven organizations,

00:13:06.934 --> 00:13:09.000
or whatever they may
be doing, against some

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:10.266
of the new behaviors.

00:13:10.266 --> 00:13:12.400
You know, I think they go and
talk to their counterparts

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:15.033
within the organizations,
and I think they tread lightly,

00:13:15.033 --> 00:13:16.734
which is like, "Hey, Catherine,
I know this isn't

00:13:16.734 --> 00:13:19.200
"my domain, but have
we thought about doing

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:20.967
"these other things," or
"What are we doing

00:13:20.967 --> 00:13:23.166
"about those things,
I'm curious as a consumer

00:13:23.166 --> 00:13:24.233
"of where we're going."

00:13:24.233 --> 00:13:26.533
I mean, again, it's just good
old-fashioned communications.

00:13:26.533 --> 00:13:29.066
But I do think it's
dangerous when you don't

00:13:29.066 --> 00:13:31.567
have cross-disciplines,
I mean, it's amazing to me

00:13:31.567 --> 00:13:33.667
how all 650 people
that work for me

00:13:33.667 --> 00:13:36.734
have plenty of ideas of
what the CEO should be doing,

00:13:36.734 --> 00:13:39.200
but it's really easy to
say that when you're not

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:41.066
the last line of defense
and you don't have

00:13:41.066 --> 00:13:43.900
all the context to why.

00:13:43.900 --> 00:13:47.500
- [Sara] What's the hardest
thing about being a CEO

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:50.200
and trying to get an
organization to change,

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:51.567
culture change?

00:13:51.567 --> 00:13:53.333
- [Gary] It's lonely. It's hard.

00:13:53.333 --> 00:13:54.934
You know, being a CEO is hard.

00:13:54.934 --> 00:13:56.867
You know, especially
when it's your business.

00:13:56.867 --> 00:13:59.133
You know, if I'm a
CEO in Corporate America,

00:13:59.133 --> 00:14:00.633
I'm a hell of a
lot more interested,

00:14:00.633 --> 00:14:02.367
because even if
you fail, you still get

00:14:02.367 --> 00:14:05.033
enormous cash
bonuses on your way out.

00:14:05.033 --> 00:14:07.000
So that's interesting.

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:08.033
That I like.

00:14:08.033 --> 00:14:10.166
Being an entrepreneur
of an independent company

00:14:10.166 --> 00:14:12.967
like myself is, if I stink,
we go out of business.

00:14:12.967 --> 00:14:14.266
And you don't get the bonuses,

00:14:14.266 --> 00:14:15.734
and you don't get the accolades.

00:14:15.734 --> 00:14:17.200
And you know, my business,

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:19.133
my life is
predicated on headaches.

00:14:19.133 --> 00:14:21.333
You know, 20 minutes
before I got here,

00:14:21.333 --> 00:14:23.834
I was in my hotel room,
here, it's so beautiful here.

00:14:23.834 --> 00:14:25.633
I don't know if you saw
the ocean, it's gorgeous.

00:14:25.633 --> 00:14:26.633
Everyone's having a good time.

00:14:26.633 --> 00:14:28.200
There's candy for
days in the back room.

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:29.233
And what am I doing?

00:14:29.233 --> 00:14:32.367
I'm in my room, dealing
with four major headaches.

00:14:32.367 --> 00:14:34.200
Problem after
problem after problem,

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:36.367
plus, I'm an HR-driven CEO,

00:14:36.367 --> 00:14:39.734
so I like to get
into the muck, meaning,

00:14:39.734 --> 00:14:42.667
I really fundamentally
care about their opinions on,

00:14:42.667 --> 00:14:44.400
you know, I'm
really the head of HR,

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:46.233
even though I just
hired a Chief Heart Officer

00:14:46.233 --> 00:14:49.600
to really
enforce HR, but you know,

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:51.834
I could be on a
call in a couple hours

00:14:51.834 --> 00:14:53.533
that's Susan, who says,

00:14:53.533 --> 00:14:56.767
"Hey Gary,
Karen's trying to ruin me."

00:14:56.767 --> 00:14:58.533
You know, that could be
like 40 minutes of my day.

00:14:58.533 --> 00:15:01.734
(audience laughter)

00:15:01.734 --> 00:15:05.633
So you know, what's
it like to be a CEO?

00:15:05.633 --> 00:15:07.233
It's lonely.

00:15:07.233 --> 00:15:10.166
Listen, it's really
fun to be able to clap up,

00:15:10.166 --> 00:15:12.266
like "Yeah, it's the
machine, it's the man."

00:15:12.266 --> 00:15:14.433
I stood in front of my
entire 75-person office

00:15:14.433 --> 00:15:16.767
in L.A. and said, "Look,
just so you understand,

00:15:16.767 --> 00:15:19.000
"everything that is
wrong with this company

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:20.967
"is my fault, all of it."

00:15:20.967 --> 00:15:22.900
Because I empower
all the other people

00:15:22.900 --> 00:15:24.667
that are screwing up, right?

00:15:24.667 --> 00:15:28.734
So, you know, how is it?

00:15:28.734 --> 00:15:30.100
For me, I love it.

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:32.567
I love the action,
I'm an entrepreneur.

00:15:32.567 --> 00:15:34.200
It's my oxygen.

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:35.166
I couldn't do anything else.

00:15:35.166 --> 00:15:36.533
I've never
worked for anybody else.

00:15:36.533 --> 00:15:38.133
I don't know another gear.

00:15:38.133 --> 00:15:39.567
It's my DNA.

00:15:39.567 --> 00:15:42.633
But it's not that easy.

00:15:42.633 --> 00:15:44.300
We're living
through the greatest era

00:15:44.300 --> 00:15:46.734
of fake entrepreneurship
right now in America,

00:15:46.734 --> 00:15:48.567
because everybody in
their 20s is popping out

00:15:48.567 --> 00:15:50.166
and they're the
next Mark Zuckerberg,

00:15:50.166 --> 00:15:52.633
and we have 99% of
those companies about to go

00:15:52.633 --> 00:15:55.000
out of business once
the next financial crisis,

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:58.000
which I hope
happens soon, comes.

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:00.333
So that we can wipe out all
this fake entrepreneurship

00:16:00.333 --> 00:16:03.033
and all this bull crap,
but you know,

00:16:03.033 --> 00:16:05.000
but it's hard.

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:07.300
You look at the universities
in America right now,

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:09.133
that have entrepreneurship
at the forefront,

00:16:09.133 --> 00:16:11.700
Babson, UPenn, they
have the highest rate

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:14.467
of suicide, because
a lot of kids right now

00:16:14.467 --> 00:16:16.000
think that it's just so easy to,

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:17.767
like, when did this happen?

00:16:17.767 --> 00:16:20.000
When did everybody
just think they can build

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:22.166
a million dollar business just
because you live in America

00:16:22.166 --> 00:16:24.400
and you're 24 and
you have a hoodie?

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:25.567
(audience laughs)
- [Sara] Right.

00:16:25.567 --> 00:16:27.400
- I should have worn a hoodie.

00:16:27.400 --> 00:16:28.734
- Yeah, we were thinking about

00:16:28.734 --> 00:16:29.700
wearing matching hoodies.

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:31.633
- But this is what's happened.

00:16:31.633 --> 00:16:34.633
Like there's a
stunning conversation

00:16:34.633 --> 00:16:36.600
because entrepreneurship
is very Americana,

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:38.300
it's very hot right now.

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:41.533
But being a
micro-industry celebrity,

00:16:41.533 --> 00:16:43.834
it's crazy to me how
much the entrepreneur is being

00:16:43.834 --> 00:16:44.934
put on a pedestal.

00:16:44.934 --> 00:16:46.700
I'm feeling it enormously.

00:16:46.700 --> 00:16:50.533
And I think it's dangerous,
because I don't think

00:16:50.533 --> 00:16:52.533
people think they
can just be LeBron James.

00:16:52.533 --> 00:16:54.233
I don't think people
think they can just be Beyonce.

00:16:54.233 --> 00:16:56.700
I don't think they think they
can just be these things,

00:16:56.700 --> 00:16:58.834
yet for some reason,
we're in this place right now,

00:16:58.834 --> 00:17:00.567
where we think we can just build

00:17:00.567 --> 00:17:02.900
these million dollar
businesses, and entrepreneurship

00:17:02.900 --> 00:17:04.834
is a skill, it's a talent.

00:17:06.500 --> 00:17:07.700
Most are born with it.

00:17:07.700 --> 00:17:11.133
You can be better at it,
but to be a great one?

00:17:11.133 --> 00:17:14.733
And then we have a crazy
conversation around income.

00:17:14.733 --> 00:17:17.800
Like the fact that the
bottom of the 1% earners

00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:19.967
in this country
make $400,000 a year,

00:17:19.967 --> 00:17:22.934
yet all these 20-year-olds
think that a million dollars

00:17:22.934 --> 00:17:26.099
a year is like the baseline
for maybe being in the game?

00:17:26.099 --> 00:17:28.767
We are just completely
in outer space right now

00:17:28.767 --> 00:17:31.333
on this conversation.

00:17:31.333 --> 00:17:32.166
- [Scott] Interesting.

00:17:32.166 --> 00:17:34.066
Someone's actually
reading your blog live,

00:17:34.066 --> 00:17:35.867
while you're on
stage, so we got a question

00:17:35.867 --> 00:17:36.734
on your blog.

00:17:36.734 --> 00:17:38.533
It says, "In your
last post, you talk about

00:17:38.533 --> 00:17:40.233
"using empathy to make a sale."
- [Gary] Yes.

00:17:40.233 --> 00:17:42.333
- "How can this
audience use empathy

00:17:42.333 --> 00:17:44.200
"in their daily business?

00:17:44.200 --> 00:17:47.400
- [Gary] I think empathy
is the greatest trait

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:49.567
that I was gifted by my mother.

00:17:49.567 --> 00:17:51.934
And I use it every day.

00:17:51.934 --> 00:17:53.834
I think it makes me
a great salesperson.

00:17:53.834 --> 00:17:56.467
I think way too many
organizations don't deploy

00:17:56.467 --> 00:17:59.467
empathy, ever, and most of all,

00:17:59.467 --> 00:18:01.900
not on their end consumer.

00:18:01.900 --> 00:18:04.633
Most of you think way
too much inside of a bubble,

00:18:04.633 --> 00:18:06.600
and you get into a room,
and you white board

00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:08.767
in things that are
in your best interest,

00:18:08.767 --> 00:18:10.800
but not in the end
consumer's best interest.

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:14.033
So empathy is
completely left at the garbage

00:18:14.033 --> 00:18:15.667
before you walk into the door.

00:18:15.667 --> 00:18:18.333
And for me, I try
to run my organization

00:18:18.333 --> 00:18:21.667
and my offerings, and
when I sold wine, and when

00:18:21.667 --> 00:18:23.667
I sell books, and when
I sell clients services,

00:18:23.667 --> 00:18:25.333
and whatever I do
for the rest of my life,

00:18:25.333 --> 00:18:28.533
my goal is always, how
do I deploy 51% of the value

00:18:28.533 --> 00:18:32.333
of this transaction
to the other party.

00:18:32.333 --> 00:18:33.400
And I use empathy.

00:18:33.400 --> 00:18:35.333
I try to understand why.

00:18:35.333 --> 00:18:38.467
This organization
needs to take a step back

00:18:38.467 --> 00:18:40.400
and understand why
nobody in America knows

00:18:40.400 --> 00:18:42.734
what a credit union is.

00:18:42.734 --> 00:18:45.400
That would be the first
step to a lot more money

00:18:45.400 --> 00:18:48.033
made for everybody in this room.

00:18:48.033 --> 00:18:51.533
Not, you're emotional
to why it's not happening.

00:18:51.533 --> 00:18:53.433
Not that it should be happening.

00:18:53.433 --> 00:18:55.867
Not succumbing
to it's happening.

00:18:55.867 --> 00:18:58.767
But understanding
why it's happening.

00:18:58.767 --> 00:18:59.934
And then that would help.

00:18:59.934 --> 00:19:01.900
And the way to do
that is to deploy empathy

00:19:01.900 --> 00:19:03.633
and understand
what's happening in people's

00:19:03.633 --> 00:19:05.967
day-in, day-out lives,
that isn't allowing us

00:19:05.967 --> 00:19:07.333
to penetrate them
with the messaging

00:19:07.333 --> 00:19:09.967
that describes
what we do for a living.

00:19:09.967 --> 00:19:11.467
- What are some of
the techniques you use

00:19:11.467 --> 00:19:15.066
to gain an empathetic
understanding of the consumer?

00:19:15.066 --> 00:19:17.200
Like if you were
in the credit unions,

00:19:17.200 --> 00:19:19.066
would you do consumer polls?

00:19:19.066 --> 00:19:21.133
Would you go talk to students?

00:19:21.133 --> 00:19:23.633
Or how do you
actually do that, you know?

00:19:23.633 --> 00:19:24.900
- [Gary] Yeah,
that's a good question.

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:27.133
So I think what
you just mentioned

00:19:27.133 --> 00:19:30.200
are non-empathetic
machines doing tactics

00:19:30.200 --> 00:19:32.433
to try to fake empathy.

00:19:32.433 --> 00:19:36.734
What I would do is try
to find empathetic people

00:19:36.734 --> 00:19:39.333
and empower them to do shit.

00:19:39.333 --> 00:19:42.166
(audience laughter)

00:19:43.066 --> 00:19:44.700
- Pretty simple.

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:46.500
- Dude, I mean.

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:47.400
I mean, I mean it.

00:19:47.400 --> 00:19:48.834
Like, I don't know.

00:19:48.834 --> 00:19:51.066
You know what's
funny about that.

00:19:51.066 --> 00:19:53.100
- I think you should
