There's 60-year-old
dudes in here

00:30:00.500 --> 00:30:02.834
who texted a
poop emoji last night.

00:30:02.834 --> 00:30:04.266
(audience laughter)

00:30:04.266 --> 00:30:06.467
I mean look at this lovely lady.

00:30:06.467 --> 00:30:08.233
She's got two
technology devices.

00:30:08.233 --> 00:30:11.633
A phone and an iPad,
and she's 80 years old!

00:30:11.633 --> 00:30:13.900
(audience laughter)

00:30:16.467 --> 00:30:17.300
Kidding.

00:30:17.300 --> 00:30:18.633
- Are you on Snapchat as well?

00:30:18.633 --> 00:30:21.166
- But the point is,

00:30:21.166 --> 00:30:22.333
and obviously I was
going for the laugh there,

00:30:22.333 --> 00:30:23.567
you look amazing.

00:30:23.567 --> 00:30:24.400
I'm going to
kiss you on the face

00:30:24.400 --> 00:30:26.367
when I get done with this talk.

00:30:26.367 --> 00:30:27.867
I want people to understand.

00:30:27.867 --> 00:30:29.400
You guys know this.

00:30:29.400 --> 00:30:31.433
Your parents are on this.

00:30:31.433 --> 00:30:32.767
This is real.

00:30:32.767 --> 00:30:36.266
How are you possibly
disrespecting this still

00:30:36.266 --> 00:30:38.367
because your
organization doesn't support

00:30:38.367 --> 00:30:39.834
digital spend or

00:30:39.834 --> 00:30:41.800
digital native activities?

00:30:41.800 --> 00:30:44.533
It's crazy. It's over.

00:30:44.533 --> 00:30:46.667
I don't care
how you want it to be.

00:30:46.667 --> 00:30:47.500
It's the way it is.

00:30:47.500 --> 00:30:50.166
And by the way, you take
the negative view on this.

00:30:50.166 --> 00:30:51.567
Let me talk to you about
something that's going on

00:30:51.567 --> 00:30:53.967
in society that I know
all of you have judged.

00:30:53.967 --> 00:30:56.667
You go out to a restaurant,
and you see a couple,

00:30:56.667 --> 00:30:58.867
and they're having dinner,
and they're both on the phone.

00:30:58.867 --> 00:31:00.867
And you just love
to say to your partner,

00:31:00.867 --> 00:31:04.133
"Hey do you see that, how sad."

00:31:04.133 --> 00:31:05.633
Right? You love that, right?

00:31:05.633 --> 00:31:07.033
You love judging, don't you?

00:31:07.033 --> 00:31:08.300
Right?

00:31:08.300 --> 00:31:09.834
Here's what I see.

00:31:09.834 --> 00:31:13.767
I see two people
that 15 years ago,

00:31:13.767 --> 00:31:15.300
would've had dinner together,

00:31:15.300 --> 00:31:16.900
would've sat
across from each other,

00:31:16.900 --> 00:31:19.934
and wouldn't have said
a damn word to each other.

00:31:19.934 --> 00:31:21.166
Remember those conversations?

00:31:21.166 --> 00:31:22.000
You've seen that.

00:31:22.000 --> 00:31:24.300
Couples sitting right
next to you, not a word.

00:31:24.300 --> 00:31:26.000
So you know
what I think this is?

00:31:26.000 --> 00:31:27.967
It's a blessing to those two!

00:31:27.967 --> 00:31:29.834
(audience laughter)

00:31:29.834 --> 00:31:31.033
It is!

00:31:31.033 --> 00:31:32.934
I'm happy for those two people

00:31:32.934 --> 00:31:34.133
that they can actually be doing

00:31:34.133 --> 00:31:38.300
what they
actually want to be doing!

00:31:38.300 --> 00:31:39.633
- True.

00:31:39.633 --> 00:31:40.800
Sam, you got a question?

00:31:40.800 --> 00:31:41.600
- [Sam] Yes.

00:31:41.600 --> 00:31:44.400
Is this thing on?
Here I am. Okay, so

00:31:44.400 --> 00:31:45.667
this is, we talk about this,

00:31:45.667 --> 00:31:47.333
that marketers,
credit union marketers,

00:31:47.333 --> 00:31:48.500
we just have to
get the story out.

00:31:48.500 --> 00:31:50.533
We have this comms challenge.

00:31:50.533 --> 00:31:53.500
But what I see a lot in
credit unions is that we do,

00:31:53.500 --> 00:31:55.533
that marketers
are held to an ROI

00:31:55.533 --> 00:31:58.000
and push this
very specific product,

00:31:58.000 --> 00:31:59.433
that they have to
take that to the board,

00:31:59.433 --> 00:32:02.533
it has to get approved, it's
like marketing by committee.

00:32:02.533 --> 00:32:06.033
We are kind of
hamstringing our marketing teams

00:32:06.033 --> 00:32:09.867
within our credit unions
that they can't just freely

00:32:09.867 --> 00:32:12.033
post on Snapchat when they
wake up in the morning and say,

00:32:12.033 --> 00:32:15.033
"I'm so jazzed to go to my
credit union, this is my story."

00:32:15.033 --> 00:32:17.467
We tamp that down.

00:32:17.467 --> 00:32:20.066
How do we shift that?

00:32:20.066 --> 00:32:24.200
- [Gary] A, that's why so few
people know about this, period.

00:32:25.266 --> 00:32:30.333
And B, you change
the rules from the top!

00:32:30.333 --> 00:32:33.300
Like again, who's
a CEO in this room?

00:32:33.300 --> 00:32:35.333
Raise your hand.

00:32:35.333 --> 00:32:37.367
Geez.

00:32:37.367 --> 00:32:39.734
One more time, just hold 'em up.

00:32:39.734 --> 00:32:41.800
It's on these lovely people.

00:32:41.800 --> 00:32:43.500
She's in charge.

00:32:43.500 --> 00:32:45.066
It's up to her.

00:32:45.066 --> 00:32:46.934
And by the way, I have empathy.

00:32:46.934 --> 00:32:49.000
Listen, I've spent
enormous amounts of time,

00:32:49.000 --> 00:32:51.300
and this is an
absolute bragging moment.

00:32:51.300 --> 00:32:53.300
I've spent
enormous amounts of time

00:32:53.300 --> 00:32:56.100
in the White House with the
President of the United States

00:32:56.100 --> 00:32:58.233
to go through the
legalities of comms

00:32:58.233 --> 00:33:01.300
for financial institutions
and other things.

00:33:01.300 --> 00:33:03.767
There is plenty
that you can be doing.

00:33:03.767 --> 00:33:06.233
It's a complete
and utter cop-out

00:33:06.233 --> 00:33:08.900
to use it on
compliance and rules.

00:33:08.900 --> 00:33:10.467
It's that you don't
understand it fully,

00:33:10.467 --> 00:33:12.800
you don't understand
the ROI, and that's fine.

00:33:12.800 --> 00:33:15.633
But there's a
very big difference

00:33:15.633 --> 00:33:18.867
between branding and
marketing, and sales.

00:33:18.867 --> 00:33:20.834
They have different functions.

00:33:20.834 --> 00:33:22.233
And way too many
people think that

00:33:22.233 --> 00:33:24.834
marketing and branding is sales.

00:33:24.834 --> 00:33:27.333
It's not and sales is important!

00:33:27.333 --> 00:33:28.934
I'm a salesman.

00:33:28.934 --> 00:33:31.166
I'm a salesman who just happens
to respect what branding

00:33:31.166 --> 00:33:34.967
and marketing does,
because it makes my job easier.

00:33:34.967 --> 00:33:37.700
Because Nike and Apple,

00:33:37.700 --> 00:33:40.066
they don't transact me.

00:33:40.066 --> 00:33:41.767
These guys don't sell me.

00:33:41.767 --> 00:33:43.433
They won me on branding.

00:33:43.433 --> 00:33:45.100
I buy it mindlessly.

00:33:50.667 --> 00:33:52.600
The other thing
that really pisses me off

00:33:52.600 --> 00:33:54.900
(audience laughter)

00:33:54.900 --> 00:33:56.500
Sorry.

00:33:56.500 --> 00:33:58.300
And we talked
about this two years ago,

00:33:58.300 --> 00:33:59.800
but I'm gonna pound it again.

00:33:59.800 --> 00:34:03.166
We lived through
a four, three, two,

00:34:03.166 --> 00:34:05.467
and it's still
lingering but not as much.

00:34:05.467 --> 00:34:07.166
Missed opportunity by you guys.

00:34:07.166 --> 00:34:09.433
We lived through a
three to four year window

00:34:09.433 --> 00:34:11.567
where the main competitor banks

00:34:11.567 --> 00:34:13.900
were the worst
evil in our society,

00:34:13.900 --> 00:34:16.833
and we didn't
pounce on the moment.

00:34:18.433 --> 00:34:20.567
It's true.

00:34:20.567 --> 00:34:23.033
And so like, to
be honest with you,

00:34:23.033 --> 00:34:26.000
that actually gives
me very little reason

00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:27.567
or hope to think
that anybody here

00:34:27.567 --> 00:34:30.333
is gonna do anything
about what I'm talking about.

00:34:30.333 --> 00:34:34.667
You missed the
greatest opening of all time.

00:34:34.667 --> 00:34:36.934
You just let it go by.

00:34:36.934 --> 00:34:38.467
Insane.

00:34:38.467 --> 00:34:40.233
- [Sara] But
I think it's still here.

00:34:40.233 --> 00:34:41.300
- Not as good!

00:34:41.300 --> 00:34:43.199
- Not as good but
definitely still there.

00:34:43.199 --> 00:34:44.500
- It's always there,
but guess what?

00:34:44.500 --> 00:34:46.900
It's gonna cost you a
hell of a lot more money.

00:34:46.900 --> 00:34:48.266
Not as easy.

00:34:48.266 --> 00:34:50.100
Like I don't want to
let them off the hook.

00:34:50.100 --> 00:34:51.300
No rainbows here.

00:34:51.300 --> 00:34:54.166
You screwed up.

00:34:54.166 --> 00:34:55.300
- [Sara] Okay, well I'm gonna be

00:34:55.300 --> 00:34:57.233
the Pollyanna and say
I think that the opportunity--

00:34:57.233 --> 00:35:00.200
- [Scott] We're getting
prompted on mics,

00:35:00.200 --> 00:35:01.533
but we can't see the audience.

00:35:01.533 --> 00:35:02.767
If we can turn
the house lights up,

00:35:02.767 --> 00:35:04.800
that'll help us a lot. (laughs)

00:35:04.800 --> 00:35:05.633
- Okay.

00:35:05.633 --> 00:35:06.433
- [Woman] Hi Gary,
question for you.

00:35:06.433 --> 00:35:08.767
First of all, it's terrifying
asking you a question.

00:35:08.767 --> 00:35:10.633
(audience laughter)

00:35:10.633 --> 00:35:13.367
Second of all,
this is not my question

00:35:13.367 --> 00:35:15.000
but it would be great if
you could run for president

00:35:15.000 --> 00:35:17.400
instead of the
selection that we have,

00:35:17.400 --> 00:35:18.600
so that's the next thing.

00:35:18.600 --> 00:35:19.767
- Can you help me on this?

00:35:19.767 --> 00:35:22.233
I was born in a foreign country,
so they won't let me go.

00:35:22.233 --> 00:35:25.500
But I promise you I
would make a run if I could.

00:35:25.500 --> 00:35:26.767
- [Woman] So my
actual question is,

00:35:26.767 --> 00:35:28.600
I'm actually a
customer of yours,

00:35:28.600 --> 00:35:30.633
I have bought wine
from your wine site.

00:35:30.633 --> 00:35:31.734
- Thank you so much.

00:35:31.734 --> 00:35:34.000
- [Woman] I actually bought it
the first time I heard you speak

00:35:34.000 --> 00:35:36.066
and I went right
out and bought some.

00:35:36.066 --> 00:35:38.433
So as a consumer of yours,

00:35:38.433 --> 00:35:41.100
I follow you
on Facebook as well.

00:35:41.100 --> 00:35:43.834
You have yet to reach
out to me as your customer

00:35:43.834 --> 00:35:45.834
and ask me why
I haven't bought from you

00:35:45.834 --> 00:35:48.934
in the last eight months.

00:35:48.934 --> 00:35:52.967
So what do you do: A, to
listen to your customers,

00:35:52.967 --> 00:35:57.166
and B, what do you do
to instill and allow change

00:35:57.166 --> 00:35:59.867
from your employees
within your own company?

00:35:59.867 --> 00:36:03.333
- So we've been
running data on remarketing

00:36:03.333 --> 00:36:04.533
for a long time at Wine Library.

00:36:04.533 --> 00:36:07.066
So Wine Library I haven't
run actively for four years,

00:36:07.066 --> 00:36:09.934
but your second point
is far more important to me

00:36:09.934 --> 00:36:12.367
because you're right,
it's all about scale ability.

00:36:12.367 --> 00:36:13.200
Right?

00:36:13.200 --> 00:36:14.667
Like, I can't do
everything myself.

00:36:14.667 --> 00:36:16.133
I can't call you, necessarily.

00:36:16.133 --> 00:36:18.700
So we ran data on remarketing to

00:36:18.700 --> 00:36:23.233
non-returning customers
over a seven year period

00:36:23.233 --> 00:36:26.667
from 1999 to 2006,
and the cadence

00:36:26.667 --> 00:36:28.367
on the recall for us

00:36:28.367 --> 00:36:29.934
is actually now one year.

00:36:29.934 --> 00:36:32.367
Like, we were finding
that too many customers,

00:36:32.367 --> 00:36:34.000
if we really
aggressively tried to get them

00:36:34.000 --> 00:36:37.233
into the re-funnel within
three, six, even nine months,

00:36:37.233 --> 00:36:38.900
which is where
I had it for a long time

00:36:38.900 --> 00:36:40.567
and I think we went
to a year a year ago,

00:36:40.567 --> 00:36:45.200
so I don't know exactly the
details on your transaction.

00:36:45.200 --> 00:36:48.233
One of the things that we
did four or five years ago

00:36:48.233 --> 00:36:49.767
is create a
thank you department,

00:36:49.767 --> 00:36:51.600
'cause I wrote a book
called The Thank You Economy,

00:36:51.600 --> 00:36:55.900
and so when you get
hit up in four months

00:36:55.900 --> 00:36:57.700
it's gonna start
with a phone call,

00:36:57.700 --> 00:37:00.233
because we think the
human element has much more ROI

00:37:00.233 --> 00:37:03.633
than the email
element, and obviously

00:37:03.633 --> 00:37:05.300
there's a lot of
ways to go about it.

00:37:05.300 --> 00:37:09.600
But at Wine Library, we
CRM the 1.2 million customers

00:37:09.600 --> 00:37:11.667
that have come through
the funnel through the years,

00:37:11.667 --> 00:37:13.934
pretty aggressively,
and re-market.

00:37:13.934 --> 00:37:15.433
We even did
something where I'm trying

00:37:15.433 --> 00:37:16.433
to call my own bull crap.

00:37:16.433 --> 00:37:17.934
We just did a
direct mailing piece,

00:37:17.934 --> 00:37:21.600
because I always,
and I mean this.

00:37:21.600 --> 00:37:24.500
I'm not a technologist, meaning

00:37:24.500 --> 00:37:26.500
I'm not obsessed with
Facebook and Snapchat.

00:37:26.500 --> 00:37:29.600
I'm obsessed with
the price of attention.

00:37:29.600 --> 00:37:30.300
Right?

00:37:30.300 --> 00:37:32.266
So like, direct
mail is expensive.

00:37:32.266 --> 00:37:34.100
I guess the stamp
went down two cents,

00:37:34.100 --> 00:37:37.900
but I'm always triple checking
if it's still ROI positive.

00:37:37.900 --> 00:37:39.133
But to answer your question,

00:37:39.133 --> 00:37:41.867
on the VaynerMedia
side where we work B to B,

00:37:41.867 --> 00:37:44.600
we have 100, 200-point
customers that we work with

00:37:44.600 --> 00:37:46.066
and we kind of
watch their social.

00:37:46.066 --> 00:37:47.166
What do they care about?

00:37:47.166 --> 00:37:48.767
We stay very high touch.

00:37:48.767 --> 00:37:50.500
On the wine e-commerce business

00:37:50.500 --> 00:37:52.233
that has a hell of
a lot more customers,

00:37:52.233 --> 00:37:55.867
our remarketing when
they're out of session starts

00:37:55.867 --> 00:37:57.100
at one year and gets more

00:37:57.100 --> 00:37:58.633
aggressive at
the two year point.

00:37:58.633 --> 00:38:00.333
But what's even
more interesting,

00:38:00.333 --> 00:38:01.400
and I'll add onto your question

00:38:01.400 --> 00:38:02.867
'cause it's the
right energy of a question,

00:38:02.867 --> 00:38:05.500
what we're doing with people
that are in the cycle lately

00:38:05.500 --> 00:38:07.266
has been even more fascinating.

00:38:07.266 --> 00:38:11.533
Which is, we are triple
downing on using technology

00:38:11.533 --> 00:38:14.533
as a gateway drug
to human interaction.

00:38:14.533 --> 00:38:16.033
So we're spending
a ton of time now

00:38:16.033 --> 00:38:18.867
mapping people's
Instagram and Twitter accounts,

00:38:18.867 --> 00:38:20.533
figuring out
what they care about,

00:38:20.533 --> 00:38:23.000
and then using that
as a proxy to get them

00:38:23.000 --> 00:38:24.567
into a phone call conversation.

00:38:24.567 --> 00:38:26.600
Like actually,
what would it look like

00:38:26.600 --> 00:38:29.567
if we had 80 phone
people that used technology

00:38:29.567 --> 00:38:32.633
and big data to create
more meaningful relationships,

00:38:32.633 --> 00:38:34.266
knowing your key events?

00:38:34.266 --> 00:38:35.300
One of the things
I'm building right now,

00:38:35.300 --> 00:38:37.000
it's actually done,
I'm just testing it

00:38:37.000 --> 00:38:39.567
is on your
account at Wine Library,

00:38:39.567 --> 00:38:41.100
if you want to give it to me,

00:38:41.100 --> 00:38:43.367
and we're gonna ask for
it very black and white.

00:38:43.367 --> 00:38:45.367
No tricks, no games,
we're gonna say,

00:38:45.367 --> 00:38:49.467
"Hey, we'd like to know the
birthdays and biggest events

00:38:49.467 --> 00:38:52.133
in your life, and if
you want to give them to us,

00:38:52.133 --> 00:38:53.934
the 10 days leading up to it,

00:38:53.934 --> 00:38:55.433
we will give you
recommendations on wine

00:38:55.433 --> 00:38:57.633
and free shipping,
predicated on anniversaries,

00:38:57.633 --> 00:38:59.233
birthdays,
things of that nature.

00:38:59.233 --> 00:39:01.700
And plenty of people will think
that's creepy and privacy,

00:39:01.700 --> 00:39:03.900
way more are gonna
think it's a service and good.

00:39:03.900 --> 00:39:06.500
And so, hacking away.

00:39:06.500 --> 00:39:08.433
Hacking away.

00:39:08.433 --> 00:39:09.567
- [Scott] It's an
interesting flip on that,

00:39:09.567 --> 00:39:10.467
and so I've done a lot of work

00:39:10.467 --> 00:39:11.433
in the financial services sector

00:39:11.433 --> 00:39:13.734
on the negative side of this.

00:39:13.734 --> 00:39:17.233
And so insurance is the
one I know the best, worst.

00:39:17.233 --> 00:39:20.300
Insurance has such a negative
conversation with the market,

00:39:20.300 --> 00:39:23.266
to the point where the
research I did in the U.S.

00:39:23.266 --> 00:39:25.633
most insurance
policy holders only speak

00:39:25.633 --> 00:39:27.367
to their provider maybe

00:39:27.367 --> 00:39:30.734
1 to 1.1 times a year.

00:39:30.734 --> 00:39:34.100
75% of those
discussions are negative.

00:39:34.100 --> 00:39:38.233
So, it means it's a claim, a
complaint, or a cancellation.

00:39:38.233 --> 00:39:39.700
That's what you get when
you think about insurance.

00:39:39.700 --> 00:39:42.133
Even as a consumer, you have
a very negative perception

00:39:42.133 --> 00:39:45.600
of that brand, so every
time you speak to that brand,

00:39:45.600 --> 00:39:47.667
it's like
asking Gary a question.

00:39:47.667 --> 00:39:49.767
(audience laughter)

00:39:49.767 --> 00:39:52.033
- Look, there's another
thing that's emerging, guys.

00:39:52.033 --> 00:39:55.467
Which is, time is
becoming the number one asset.

00:39:55.467 --> 00:39:56.333
Right?

00:39:56.333 --> 00:39:58.000
Besides health and
well-being and money,

00:39:58.000 --> 00:40:00.600
time is emerged as one of the
core things in our pillar.

00:40:00.600 --> 00:40:03.467
So, one of the crazy
things, back to the phone thing

00:40:03.467 --> 00:40:05.433
that's interesting,
that we're trying to do,

00:40:05.433 --> 00:40:07.166
we're fighting a
trend that I believe.

00:40:07.166 --> 00:40:08.066
Watch this.

00:40:08.066 --> 00:40:12.066
By show of hands, and remember
that lying is the devil,

00:40:12.066 --> 00:40:16.800
how many people here are now
mad when another human being

00:40:16.800 --> 00:40:19.266
calls them on the phone?

00:40:19.266 --> 00:40:20.533
Raise your hand.

00:40:20.533 --> 00:40:23.066
Raise it high.

00:40:23.066 --> 00:40:24.400
This is super interesting now.

00:40:24.400 --> 00:40:26.500
We're in a place now
where technology has gotten

00:40:26.500 --> 00:40:28.166
into a place where
you don't understand

00:40:28.166 --> 00:40:29.934
why somebody's calling you.

00:40:29.934 --> 00:40:31.500
They should email or text you,

00:40:31.500 --> 00:40:35.367
and you will get
back to them on your time.

00:40:35.367 --> 00:40:36.800
I mean, literally.

00:40:36.800 --> 00:40:38.033
And so,

00:40:38.033 --> 00:40:40.300
we're just living through
a lot of changes in our...

00:40:40.300 --> 00:40:43.033
Guys, this is the
single biggest culture shift

00:40:43.033 --> 00:40:47.567
of communication in our
society in the last 70 years.

00:40:47.567 --> 00:40:49.367
I mean, this is a big
deal, this transformation

00:40:49.367 --> 00:40:51.734
to the internet age
and to the mobile age.

00:40:51.734 --> 00:40:55.633
This is a major shift
and has massive repercussions

00:40:55.633 --> 00:40:57.867
on our businesses.

00:40:57.867 --> 00:40:59.567
- [Sara] I mean, what do you
think is the biggest opportunity

00:40:59.567 --> 00:41:03.433
for credit unions right
now, given this mobile shift?

00:41:03.433 --> 00:41:06.800
- Well first, very honestly,
what I'd like

00:41:06.800 --> 00:41:09.333
to do is look
under the hood on a P&amp;amp;L

00:41:09.333 --> 00:41:12.033
in one of these
CEOs and understand

00:41:12.033 --> 00:41:13.767
what they spend their money on

00:41:13.767 --> 00:41:16.533
to try to make the thing that
they want to make happen.

00:41:16.533 --> 00:41:18.133
So I think the
biggest opportunity is,

00:41:18.133 --> 00:41:20.367
when's the last
time everybody here,

00:41:20.367 --> 00:41:21.867
who runs one of
these businesses,

00:41:21.867 --> 00:41:24.700
sat down and looked
at every dollar deployed

00:41:24.700 --> 00:41:27.066
on people,
infrastructure, marketing,

00:41:27.066 --> 00:41:30.200
the whole nine, and said what
does this penny do for me

00:41:30.200 --> 00:41:32.367
to get to what I want.

00:41:32.367 --> 00:41:36.066
The problem is
that 80% of the behavior

00:41:36.066 --> 00:41:38.967
that is being done
by companies is things

00:41:38.967 --> 00:41:40.767
that have been
accepted as tried and true,

00:41:40.767 --> 00:41:42.467
or costs of business,
or just things

00:41:42.467 --> 00:41:43.800
that they're accustomed to.

00:41:43.800 --> 00:41:47.166
In that 80%, 50% of
that stuff is vulnerable.

00:41:47.166 --> 00:41:50.100
I'd like to take that 50% and
get them to start using it

00:41:50.100 --> 00:41:52.500
against modern
behavior that can lead

00:41:52.500 --> 00:41:55.433
to a funnel of acquisition
of the kind of clientele

00:41:55.433 --> 00:41:56.633
that they're
going to be looking for

00:41:56.633 --> 00:42:00.900
for them to be in
business over the next decade.

00:42:00.900 --> 00:42:02.500
That's what I would do.
Audit.

00:42:02.500 --> 00:42:05.166
Who's got a question?
Where are the mics?

00:42:07.100 --> 00:42:08.233
- Question here
for you, the audience,

00:42:08.233 --> 00:42:10.834
does WineLibrary.com
have a mobile app?

00:42:10.834 --> 00:42:13.400
No, so we're mobile optimized,

00:42:13.400 --> 00:42:15.500
but we don't have a mobile app.

00:42:15.500 --> 00:42:17.734
Truth be told,
it's really interesting.

00:42:17.734 --> 00:42:20.133
We've A/B tested
that a little bit

00:42:20.133 --> 00:42:23.133
but I'm not
running the business day in

00:42:23.133 --> 00:42:24.700
and day out anymore,
and to air a little bit

00:42:24.700 --> 00:42:29.400
of dirty laundry, my dad,
my dad is a cliche operator

00:42:29.400 --> 00:42:31.900
that bothers me
the way he operates.

00:42:31.900 --> 00:42:32.934
I'm not there every day.

00:42:32.934 --> 00:42:34.433
It's a family business,
and as you can imagine,

00:42:34.433 --> 00:42:37.233
there's an enormous emotion
around family businesses.

00:42:37.233 --> 00:42:39.767
He ran it for a while.
I ran it for a while,

00:42:39.767 --> 00:42:41.266
changed the
landscape of the business.

00:42:41.266 --> 00:42:43.667
I'm doing other things,
and even though my dad knows

00:42:43.667 --> 00:42:46.800
what I do for a living, and
grow other people's businesses,

00:42:46.800 --> 00:42:49.367
he's pretty steadfast on
doing certain things the way

00:42:49.367 --> 00:42:50.934
he wanted to do them.

00:42:50.934 --> 00:42:53.367
It has not worked over
the last three or four years,

00:42:53.367 --> 00:42:56.233
and now, finally, I'm back to
like, doing certain things.

00:42:56.233 --> 00:42:58.900
So, like, that's
what's happening there.

00:42:58.900 --> 00:42:59.834
- [Sara] Interesting.

00:42:59.834 --> 00:43:02.600
This kind of reminds me of
when you were talking before

00:43:02.600 --> 00:43:05.900
about like, so what the world
is change, just get with it.

00:43:05.900 --> 00:43:06.400
- Right.

00:43:06.400 --> 00:43:07.800
- Where's the point
where you throw the baby out

00:43:07.800 --> 00:43:09.834
with the bathwater
in terms of wisdom that

00:43:09.834 --> 00:43:12.300
that older generation brings,
and sort of like the way

00:43:12.300 --> 00:43:13.600
we used to do things.

00:43:13.600 --> 00:43:15.367
There is a wisdom that you have

00:43:15.367 --> 00:43:17.767
to carry with you as you adapt.

00:43:17.767 --> 00:43:19.300
Where's the line there for you?

00:43:19.300 --> 00:43:21.200
- There's no blanket statement
that would answer this.

00:43:21.200 --> 00:43:22.934
One more time, how
many CEOs in this room,

00:43:22.934 --> 00:43:24.233
raise your hand.

00:43:24.233 --> 00:43:26.867
You know, do I believe 50% of
these people should be fired?

00:43:26.867 --> 00:43:27.700
Yes.

00:43:27.700 --> 00:43:29.166
(audience laughter)

00:43:29.166 --> 00:43:32.100
But I just don't
know which 50%, right?

00:43:32.100 --> 00:43:34.000
And what I mean by
that is, in the scenario,

00:43:34.000 --> 00:43:35.867
obviously I'm
going for shock effect,

00:43:35.867 --> 00:43:38.567
with saying that to the people
that just raised their hands,

00:43:38.567 --> 00:43:40.934
but the truth is,
it's the ones in this room

00:43:40.934 --> 00:43:43.266
who've used
wisdom and convert it

00:43:43.266 --> 00:43:46.266
into romance and being romantic

00:43:46.266 --> 00:43:47.800
about how you do
things is the quickest way

00:43:47.800 --> 00:43:49.300
to go out of business.

00:43:49.300 --> 00:43:51.800
I just don't know
which ones are romantic

00:43:51.800 --> 00:43:55.166
and only
leveraging wisdom, right,

00:43:55.166 --> 00:43:57.767
and I don't know which ones
are just not knowledgeable,

00:43:57.767 --> 00:44:00.233
what the alternatives
are and they're open to it,

00:44:00.233 --> 00:44:02.033
but their partners
or their people

00:44:02.033 --> 00:44:04.834
in their organization
haven't broken through to them

00:44:04.834 --> 00:44:06.233
and put them in that position.

00:44:06.233 --> 00:44:07.834
- [Sara] Yeah, has there
been a moment with you

00:44:07.834 --> 00:44:10.967
and your father, where he has
sort of shown you the light

00:44:10.967 --> 00:44:12.567
on something,
where you were like,

00:44:12.567 --> 00:44:13.967
oh my new ways aren't working.

00:44:13.967 --> 00:44:15.834
- Yeah, not when I...

00:44:15.834 --> 00:44:17.767
So look, I mean, I
took over my dad's business.

00:44:17.767 --> 00:44:20.333
It was doing $3 million
a year on 10% gross profit,

00:44:20.333 --> 00:44:23.066
which means it was
making $300,000 before expenses,

00:44:23.066 --> 00:44:25.233
and in five years,
with no cash infusion,

00:44:25.233 --> 00:44:28.633
I grew it from
$3 to $65 million in revenue.

00:44:28.633 --> 00:44:30.266
Like, I know what
the hell I'm doing.

00:44:30.266 --> 00:44:31.600
But did my dad teach me things?

00:44:31.600 --> 00:44:33.400
Of course, when
I was 16 years old,

00:44:33.400 --> 00:44:36.300
my dad grabbed me
by the arm and said,

00:44:36.300 --> 00:44:38.367
"You just bought
your first wine order,"

00:44:38.367 --> 00:44:40.734
as a 16-year-old, bought a
bunch of cases of something.

00:44:40.734 --> 00:44:44.367
And he looked at me and
said, "If you change your mind

00:44:44.367 --> 00:44:47.367
"between today and
when the order comes in,

00:44:47.367 --> 00:44:49.300
"you'll drink every
one of those bottles.

00:44:49.300 --> 00:44:51.033
"You will not
cancel that order."

00:44:51.033 --> 00:44:53.633
And he said (spits) this
is the most important thing

00:44:53.633 --> 00:44:56.333
in life, and it was
the best piece of advice

00:44:56.333 --> 00:44:58.633
I've ever given,
because, as you can tell,

00:44:58.633 --> 00:45:01.400
I'm a more refined
version of myself at 40.

00:45:01.400 --> 00:45:04.333
Imagine how full
of crap I was at 16.

00:45:04.333 --> 00:45:09.433
So, so, so sure, but,

00:45:09.433 --> 00:45:11.266
but, listen,
at the end of the day,

00:45:11.266 --> 00:45:13.600
results are results, you know?

00:45:13.600 --> 00:45:15.533
I knew what I was doing.

00:45:15.533 --> 00:45:17.133
There's a reason the
business has been flat

00:45:17.133 --> 00:45:18.200
since I've left.

00:45:18.200 --> 00:45:19.900
There's a reason that
in the last four years,

00:45:19.900 --> 00:45:23.166
that I've run
VaynerMedia it's grown

00:45:23.166 --> 00:45:25.767
from $3 to
$100 million in revenue.

00:45:25.767 --> 00:45:30.367
Like, I've got plenty at stake
along with the showmanship

00:45:30.367 --> 00:45:32.567
that I deploy here on stage.

00:45:32.567 --> 00:45:36.834
I just happen to do
both, but I, not really.

00:45:36.834 --> 00:45:38.667
Most of the arguments
my dad and I had in

00:45:38.667 --> 00:45:40.700
that eight year period,
where I really ran it,

00:45:40.700 --> 00:45:42.800
I won almost every one of them.

00:45:42.800 --> 00:45:44.667
That's just the truth.

00:45:44.667 --> 00:45:48.066
Because my dad is
romantic and emotional,

00:45:48.066 --> 00:45:49.367
and I'm not.

00:45:49.367 --> 00:45:51.734
'Cause I know the market
doesn't give a crap about me.

00:45:51.734 --> 00:45:53.266
- [Sara] Yeah, interesting.

00:45:53.266 --> 00:45:56.066
- We have a question over here.

00:45:56.066 --> 00:45:59.500
- [Man] Gary,
first a blatant suck up.

00:45:59.500 --> 00:46:01.900
I agree with you
completely about the results

00:46:01.900 --> 00:46:06.700
of 2008 to the current period,

00:46:06.700 --> 00:46:09.333
but I think the reason is,
and with all due respect

00:46:09.333 --> 00:46:11.867
to Co-op, that

00:46:11.867 --> 00:46:14.734
we've got a bifurcated industry.

00:46:14.734 --> 00:46:17.133
We have two trade organizations,

00:46:17.133 --> 00:46:21.000
we have multiple
plastic card services,

00:46:21.000 --> 00:46:24.667
we have multiple
networks, we don't talk

00:46:24.667 --> 00:46:27.934
with one voice to
the American public.

00:46:27.934 --> 00:46:29.900
There are 6500 credit unions.

00:46:29.900 --> 00:46:35.000
We talk with 6500 plus voices
and we get lost in the noise.

00:46:35.000 --> 00:46:39.266
- [Gary] I think that's an
easy way to pass on the buck.

00:46:39.266 --> 00:46:43.400
My belief is that, one
of the top 10 credit unions

00:46:43.400 --> 00:46:47.133
actually did the actions and
did the marketing themselves,

00:46:47.133 --> 00:46:50.934
that they could have
dragged everybody along.

00:46:50.934 --> 00:46:53.567
I mean, why didn't they?

00:46:53.567 --> 00:46:56.233
The truth is, I don't
want to pontificate on stage.

00:46:56.233 --> 00:46:58.800
I don't know, maybe they thought
that they were associated

00:46:58.800 --> 00:47:01.233
with financial
institutions, I'm not sure.

00:47:01.233 --> 00:47:03.200
Maybe it wasn't in their
financial vested interest,

00:47:03.200 --> 00:47:05.734
maybe they didn't understand
how to make YouTube videos

00:47:05.734 --> 00:47:08.633
and Google Adwords
during that time, I don't know.

00:47:08.633 --> 00:47:11.133
But what I do know is
that it didn't happen.

00:47:11.133 --> 00:47:12.266
And what I do know is

00:47:12.266 --> 00:47:15.900
I've never seen a
co-op move an industry.

00:47:15.900 --> 00:47:18.900
I've seen leaders within
the industry do something.

00:47:18.900 --> 00:47:22.033
It worked and then
everybody followed it.

00:47:22.667 --> 00:47:25.934
(audience applause)

00:47:25.934 --> 00:47:27.867
- So we've spoken about,
on the negative side,

00:47:27.867 --> 00:47:30.533
what they're not doing,
what's a company

00:47:30.533 --> 00:47:35.100
in the financial services
sector doing a good job?

00:47:35.100 --> 00:47:37.934
- I'm not sure.

00:47:37.934 --> 00:47:40.400
What I know is this.

00:47:40.400 --> 00:47:43.233
Here's what I know, and a lot
of this is headline reading,

00:47:43.233 --> 00:47:46.033
and I'm scared of
that, so all my answers are

00:47:46.033 --> 00:47:47.700
about to become
completely unofficial,

00:47:47.700 --> 00:47:49.533
because I hate anointing things,

00:47:49.533 --> 00:47:51.800
if I don't know
things under the hood.

00:47:51.800 --> 00:47:53.934
But I do know from
a branding standpoint

00:47:53.934 --> 00:47:56.233
that AmEX's Small
Business Saturday is the kind

00:47:56.233 --> 00:47:58.300
of way we need to
be thinking about it.

00:47:58.300 --> 00:47:59.834
Like, the data
that I've looked at,

00:47:59.834 --> 00:48:01.667
and I don't know how
successful it was compared

00:48:01.667 --> 00:48:03.934
to what they spent
against it to make it happen,

00:48:03.934 --> 00:48:06.433
but when you look
at conversion rates

00:48:06.433 --> 00:48:08.633
of small businesses
who are not happy

00:48:08.633 --> 00:48:11.266
with how much
percentage AmEX takes,

00:48:11.266 --> 00:48:14.033
and had a lot of energy
to push you towards Visa

00:48:14.033 --> 00:48:16.500
and Mastercard, over
the last seven years,

00:48:16.500 --> 00:48:18.767
of that Small Business
Saturday campaign,

00:48:18.767 --> 00:48:20.700
they've been able
to win more accounts

00:48:20.700 --> 00:48:22.800
in small businesses
around the country

00:48:22.800 --> 00:48:25.333
on the branding that
they're small business friendly.

00:48:25.333 --> 00:48:27.400
Even though they're
still taking 1.3% more

00:48:27.400 --> 00:48:29.734
of the transaction
than their competitors.

00:48:29.734 --> 00:48:32.333
So that, to me, is interesting.

00:48:32.333 --> 00:48:33.633
That to me is interesting.

00:48:33.633 --> 00:48:38.400
I think when you look at
a firm, a new organization

00:48:38.400 --> 00:48:40.900
that let's people
pay on websites

00:48:40.900 --> 00:48:44.367
in installments
instead of one transaction,

00:48:44.367 --> 00:48:48.333
when you look at how
PayPal did customer acquisition,

00:48:48.333 --> 00:48:51.734
there's things out there,
Mint, and things out there,

00:48:51.734 --> 00:48:53.166
there's certain
things out there,

00:48:53.166 --> 00:48:56.767
but the truth is I don't
spend a lot of time looking

00:48:56.767 --> 00:49:00.000
at what people are doing
outside of me or my clients.

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:01.834
Even the
competitors of my clients.

00:49:01.834 --> 00:49:05.367
So I'm not sure,
because my North Star

00:49:05.367 --> 00:49:09.266
for doing something well isn't,
you liked their commercial,

00:49:09.266 --> 00:49:12.233
or you liked their campaign,
or you thought this bank

00:49:12.233 --> 00:49:15.500
or this credit union
did a nice job on Instagram.

00:49:15.500 --> 00:49:18.133
I want to know what that
money that they spent on,

00:49:18.133 --> 00:49:20.500
meant to their
bottom line as a business.

00:49:20.500 --> 00:49:22.834
And without looking
under somebody's hood,

00:49:22.834 --> 00:49:25.000
and understanding that,
I just don't know.

00:49:25.000 --> 00:49:26.800
- [Scott] So would I be
right in saying you don't

00:49:26.800 --> 00:49:28.867
currently have a
financial services client?

00:49:28.867 --> 00:49:32.300
- So, we do, but
I'm under a lot of NDAs.

00:49:33.500 --> 00:49:35.467
We have a new one,
a big one, we have one

00:49:35.467 --> 00:49:36.867
of the three top banks.

00:49:36.867 --> 00:49:38.867
We literally,
ironically today while I was

00:49:38.867 --> 00:49:40.300
in the parking lot,
won a massive piece

00:49:40.300 --> 00:49:41.367
of business from them.

00:49:41.367 --> 00:49:42.400
We were working with one

00:49:42.400 --> 00:49:45.266
of the top four credit
card companies in America.

00:49:45.266 --> 00:49:49.467
We didn't see the
world the same way,

00:49:49.467 --> 00:49:52.367
and I'm super rich already.

00:49:52.367 --> 00:49:55.000
I don't need clients that
aren't gonna do the behavior

00:49:55.000 --> 00:49:57.066
that is interesting
for me to learn from,

00:49:57.066 --> 00:50:00.467
so that one ended.

00:50:00.467 --> 00:50:01.433
- [Scott] Interesting.

00:50:01.433 --> 00:50:03.834
Who here would like to be a
Gary Vaynerchuk client now?

00:50:03.834 --> 00:50:06.333
(laughs)
(audience laughs)

00:50:06.333 --> 00:50:07.166
- [Sara] There's a question.

00:50:07.166 --> 00:50:09.533
- Question over here?

00:50:09.533 --> 00:50:10.700
- [Man 2] Yeah,
Gary, you've indicated

00:50:10.700 --> 00:50:14.000
that you think that
being emotional and a romantic

00:50:14.000 --> 00:50:15.400
is a fatal flaw--
- Yes.

00:50:15.400 --> 00:50:18.166
- [Man 2] in a business, but
that you rely heavily on

00:50:18.166 --> 00:50:19.633
being empathetic.
- [Gary] Yes.

00:50:19.633 --> 00:50:22.300
- Can you help me parse those
two concepts a little bit?

00:50:22.300 --> 00:50:24.600
- [Gary] Yeah,
they're contradictions.

00:50:24.600 --> 00:50:26.834
So, way to sniff it out.

00:50:26.834 --> 00:50:29.533
(audience laughter)

00:50:29.533 --> 00:50:30.967
You know, I think that,

00:50:30.967 --> 00:50:34.867
I think that
way too many people,

00:50:34.867 --> 00:50:37.700
when I say
emotional and romantic,

00:50:37.700 --> 00:50:40.600
they are not respecting
what the market is doing.

00:50:40.600 --> 00:50:42.633
I actually think they're
completely opposite things.

00:50:42.633 --> 00:50:46.633
I use empathy to try to figure
out what the market is doing,

00:50:46.633 --> 00:50:49.700
and then give them
what they want, and I define

00:50:49.700 --> 00:50:53.133
being romantic and
emotional about things is,

00:50:53.133 --> 00:50:55.467
something's happening that's
not in your best interest

00:50:55.467 --> 00:50:58.333
in the market, but you
don't want it to happen,

00:50:58.333 --> 00:51:01.667
so you create a delusional
conversation with yourself,

00:51:01.667 --> 00:51:03.233
making pretend
it doesn't happen.

00:51:03.233 --> 00:51:08.266
Or worse, and this
one really is a big one.

00:51:08.266 --> 00:51:12.834
Or worse, you as a
human being recognize

00:51:12.834 --> 00:51:15.133
that you're not
gonna be in that company

00:51:15.133 --> 00:51:17.533
two or three years
from now, and taking money

00:51:17.533 --> 00:51:21.100
and investing in the
logo's health in a decade

00:51:21.100 --> 00:51:24.200
is gonna lower the margin
and profits for the company

00:51:24.200 --> 00:51:27.133
over the next two years,
which means when you leave it,

00:51:27.133 --> 00:51:30.533
you don't leave with
as much money personally

00:51:30.533 --> 00:51:32.467
because you
